WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.603
The agent reads your request conservatively.

2
00:00:02.883 --> 00:00:15.619
The Claude Code best-practices page names a pattern you’ve probably seen: Claude tends to read a request conservatively and build the smallest thing that satisfies it, because it can’t see how much you were hoping for.

3
00:00:15.899 --> 00:00:24.923
Its other failure is the opposite: confidently building the wrong thing, because the request was ambiguous and it picked an interpretation without asking.

4
00:00:25.203 --> 00:00:26.781
Both come from the same gap.

5
00:00:27.061 --> 00:00:31.819
The request didn’t contain the information the agent needed, and the agent filled it in.

6
00:00:32.099 --> 00:00:36.152
Planning closes that gap before code exists, when changes are cheap.

7
00:00:36.432 --> 00:00:38.139
You already have the skills for this.

8
00:00:38.419 --> 00:00:50.173
A PM who writes a crisp ticket, with a goal, acceptance criteria, and what’s out of scope, gets better work from engineers; the same ticket gets better work from an agent, for the same reasons.

9
00:00:50.453 --> 00:01:03.659
Simon Willison makes the point directly in his list of practices that make coding agents effective: planning, clear instructions, good tests, and review are management skills, and people with them get more out of agents.
